Two fixes to MAME's i386 core, found while getting the SunOS 4.0.1 DOS window
to run on the Sun386i. Measured against MAME 0.289.


1. A task switch must reach the GDT and the TSSes as the supervisor
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

i386_task_switch() and i286_task_switch() read and write the descriptor tables
and both task state segments through the READ/WRITE macros, and in i386.h those
are

    inline uint32_t READ32(uint32_t ea) { return READ32PL(ea, m_CPL); }

so every one of those accesses is made at the privilege level the processor
happens to be running at. On real hardware they are implicit accesses by the
control unit and are always made as the supervisor, whatever the CPL is.

It goes unnoticed until something starts a task switch from ring 3. SunOS 4.0.1
on the Sun386i does exactly that, and it is how it runs DOS: the kernel maps the
process its own task state segment at user address 0x110000, leaves the nested
task flag set and the back link pointing at it, and the DOS emulator then enters
virtual 8086 mode with a plain

    mov eax, esp
    iretd
    mov esp, eax

An IRET with NT set is a task return, so that single instruction switches tasks
with no system call in the way, which is what made it fast in 1988. Under MAME
the switch began and never finished. The first thing it touches is the busy bit
in the outgoing task's descriptor, at gdt+0x5d, which lives on a kernel only
page: at CPL 3 the translation fails, a page fault is thrown, and because MAME
raises faults with a C++ throw the exception leaves i386_task_switch() from the
middle with nothing logged. The process died with SIGSEGV on the iretd.

Measured: probe iret pc=00120504 cpl=3 nt=1 tr=0058 link=00000088, then
probe fault 14 err=00000005 cr2=fc0b1c81 cpl=3.

The rest of the file already gets this right; i386_load_protected_mode_segment()
reads descriptors with READ32PL(base + entry, 0). The fix makes the two task
switch routines do the same.


2. Only ring 0 may return to virtual 8086 mode
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

i386_protected_mode_iret() decides to return to virtual 8086 mode from the VM
bit of the stacked EFLAGS alone. The manual takes that path only when CPL is
also 0; at any outer level the bit is ignored and an ordinary protected mode
return is performed. As it stood, a user mode IRET could put the processor into
virtual 8086 mode by itself, tearing another 24 bytes off its own stack on the
way. The same omission let an outer ring load the VM bit through the two
ordinary return paths, so those now keep the old value.

This one is real but it is not what broke the DOS window: with the nested task
flag set the IRET never reaches that branch.


3. The 386 is built without a coprocessor, and without its dispatch table
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

i386_device::device_start() calls

    build_opcode_table(OP_I386);

while every later model passes OP_FPU as well and then also calls
build_x87_opcode_table(). So on the plain i386 the escape opcodes are not
decoded at all: floating point does nothing and silently returns rubbish.

On the guest it looks like this, and it took three wrong guesses to get here:

    # echo hi | awk '{i=1; print "in rule i=[" i "]"}'
    in rule i=[1.7762666567561593069e-307]
    # echo hi | awk '{print 2+2}'
    1.7763922789070136998e-307
    # echo hi | awk '{i="abc"} END{print "i=[" i "]"}'
    i=[abc]
    # expr 2 + 2
    4

Strings are fine and the shell's own integer arithmetic is fine; everything
that goes through a double is not, because awk keeps its numbers as doubles.
It is what stopped the SunOS 4.0.2 upgrade: install_update works out whether
the root and /usr file systems are local with an awk pipeline, and got an
empty answer, so `expr` was handed a bare plus sign.

Adding OP_FPU on its own segfaults MAME: the x87 dispatch table is still all
nulls. Both calls are needed.


4. The 386 never resets the x87
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

i386_device::device_reset() does not call x87_reset(); every model from the
486 on does. The stack comes up tagged valid rather than empty, so the first
fld overflows it. Software would normally not notice because the firmware
runs FNINIT at power on, which brings us to the last one.


5. The board has to say a 387 is fitted (not a MAME bug)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The SunOS kernel decides whether there is a coprocessor from one bit and
nothing else, in initfp:

    mov edx, cr0
    test edx, 0x10          ; CR0.ET
    jne mathchip_387
    jmp nofp

On a 386 that bit is set by the board when a 387 is present, and MAME's i386
comes up with CR0 = 0x7fffffe0, so ET is clear. That is a fair default for a
bare 386, but this machine has the chip, so the stub PROM now sets ET along
with PE when it enters protected mode: `or al, 0x11` instead of `or al, 1`.
Without it the kernel takes the nofp path and never initialises or saves the
coprocessor, whatever MAME does.
